// ── GRID SIZE, CHOSEN AT LOAD (2026-09-16). G cannot be a live verb: the world program is a TEMPLATE LITERAL
//   built once by makeScripts() at page load, and G is woven through it — the ring genome (gridR: G>>1), every
//   λ grid, the GPU textures, the canvas elements. So the ⊞G control is a TWO-PHASE verb: the choice is written
//   to REPLICATED register state (register.gridG), every peer sees it on the shared beat, and each then RELOADS
//   with ?G=<n> so its next world program is built at the new size. The URL is what carries the choice across
//   the reload — module state does not survive it, and a joiner arriving later reads G from its own URL too.
//   WHY NOT A BARE MODULE CONSTANT: it was one, and changing it meant editing source. This keeps the
//   determinism property that mattered (G is fixed for the life of a world program, identical on every peer
//   BY CONSTRUCTION) while making the choice reachable from the UI.
//   G_CHOICES: powers of two, 2 → 512. Below 32 the medium degenerates and the UI says so rather than hiding it —
//   MEASURED on the Fresnel operator (base pinned at the G=128 span, 2026-09-24): radii ≥ G/2 are dropped (mu1's physics
//   guard) and the cascade's radii start at 6 (the minDelay floor), so G=2/4/8 keep NO ring (lap9 only) and G=16 keeps 0-2
//   bands — the coarse/fine split then has nothing on one side and ⌗c/⌗f silently becomes lap9-only. From G=32 up the medium
//   is the SAME at every size: 4 bands, knee median 0.185, a cycle every 14.34 ticks, 2.33 beats per tick — a cell is a
//   fixed physical size and G is zoom (finding_g_as_zoom_not_quality). Offered anyway: a degenerate medium is honest to see.
const G_CHOICES = [2, 4, 8, 16, 32, 64, 128, 256, 512];
const G_DEFAULT = 128;
// _urlInt(name, dflt, ok) — read an INTEGER world parameter from the page URL at module load.
//   THE SHARED SHAPE for every such parameter (G, SUB, CPU_SUB): the value is read ONCE, here, and baked into
//   the world program every peer builds — so the initial state is identical on all of them BY CONSTRUCTION,
//   exactly as if it were a hand-edited constant. That is what makes a URL parameter admissible where a boot
//   VERB is not: a verb fires at a peer-local moment and a joiner would restore mid-transition.
//   `ok` VALIDATES, and it is not optional politeness — an out-of-range value would produce a world no other
//   peer can build, so a malformed URL must fall back to the default rather than invent one.
//   The URL is also the honest place for these: they are properties of the WORLD, so "this link opens a world
//   with these parameters" is exactly right, and everyone following the link gets the same world.
const _urlInt = (name, dflt, ok) => {
  try {
    //   ── CASE-INSENSITIVE (2026-09-17, user: "some of them are upper letters, but the url turns them into
    //   'sub' and it does not work"). URLSearchParams.get() IS case-sensitive, so ?sub=16 misses "SUB" and
    //   falls through to the default — SILENTLY, which is exactly why it read as "the parameter is ignored".
    //   Anything that normalises a pasted link (a chat client, an editor, a browser autocomplete, typing it by
    //   hand) can lowercase the query and every one of these parameters quietly stops working.
    //   So match on the lowercased KEY. The names stay upper-case in docs and in the HUD because that is what
    //   they are called in the source; the reader simply does not care how they were typed.
    const sp = new URLSearchParams(globalThis.location?.search || "");
    const want = String(name).toLowerCase();
    let q = null;
    for (const [k, v] of sp) { if (String(k).toLowerCase() === want) { q = v; } }   // last wins, as URLSearchParams.get does for repeats
    if (q !== null && q !== "" && Number.isFinite(+q)) { const n = +q | 0; if (ok(n)) return n; }
  } catch (e) {}
  return dflt;
};
//   _urlNum — _urlInt for a REAL parameter (?GAMMA=, ?WDT=): same case-insensitive read, no integer truncation.
const _urlNum = (name, dflt, ok) => {
  try { const sp = new URLSearchParams(globalThis.location?.search || ""), want = String(name).toLowerCase(); let q = null;
    for (const [k, v] of sp) { if (String(k).toLowerCase() === want) q = v; }
    if (q !== null && q !== "" && Number.isFinite(+q) && ok(+q)) return +q;
  } catch (e) {}
  return dflt; };
//   _urlKey() — is the room explicitly named with ?k=? The app does not interpret the value; it only needs to
//   know whether the user named the room (see `selo`).
//   EXACT-KEY, deliberately, unlike _urlInt: selo.html composes the id with `params.get('k')`, which is
//   case-SENSITIVE. Matching ?K= loosely here would drop our suffix while selo.html ignored the key — the room
//   would silently lose BOTH the parameters and the name. Whatever this returns must agree with what
//   selo.html will actually do, so it reads the same exact key.
const _urlKey = () => {
  try { const v = new URLSearchParams(globalThis.location?.search || "").get("k");
    return (v === null || v === "") ? null : v;
  } catch (e) { return null; }
};
